      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "The analysis of variance (ANOVA) is widely used throughout this book, e.g., in estimating variance components under balanced experimental designs (Chapters 18–24) and in detecting QTLs via marker-trait associations (Chapters 14–16). ANOVA designs are typically based upon sums of squares (SS_x) and their associated mean squares (MS_x = SS_x / n_x, with n_x the associated degrees of freedom). ANOVA test statistics are generally given by ratios of mean squares, F = MS_x / MS_y. Under normality assumptions, sums of squares are $ \\chi^2 $-distributed, while under the null hypothesis F is distributed as a (central) F-ratio distribution with $ n_x $ (numerator) and $ n_y $ (denominator) degrees of freedom, and we denote this as $ F \\sim F_{n_x, n_y} $. Thus, a test of level $ \\alpha $ for the hypothesis of no additional effects on level x (compared to level y) is whether $ F > F_{n_x, n_y, [1-\\alpha]} $, where $ F_{n_x, n_y, [\\alpha]} $ satisfies",
