    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In order to compute this probability, we require the distribution of the test statistic F under the alternative hypothesis. Here, some sums of squares follow a noncentral $ \\chi^{2} $ distribution, and the resulting F statistic involving these follows a noncentral F distribution. Hence, before considering power, we describe the properties of central and noncentral $ \\chi^{2} $ and F distributions, proofs of which can be found in standard texts such as Scheffé (1959), Searle (1971), and Johnson and Kotz (1970b).",
